#!/bin/sh
# CLASS-A HISTORY-REWRITE GUARD — #158.
#
# Refuses any change to a local branch in a shared working tree that would
# leave a commit reachable from NOTHING -- whether by moving the branch or by
# deleting it. That is the harm class observed in the libs/iridium incident: two
# seats writing one tree, one seat's amend destroying the other's commit,
# with NO PUSH ANYWHERE IN THE HARM PATH. Branch protection cannot see this
# class at all -- the damage completes before anything reaches a remote --
# so this hook is AUTHORITATIVE here, not advisory.
#
# ⚠️ THIS SUMMARY SAID "REFUSES ANY NON-FAST-FORWARD MOVE" until 2026-08-08.
# Corrected AT THE TOP as well as at the predicate, because ⭐ THIS IS THE LINE
# A READER STOPS AT, so a stale sentence here outranks a correct one 300 lines
# down. Two things changed: the rule is orphaning rather than non-fast-forward,
# and deletion is in scope.
#
# WHY THIS HOOK AND NOT prepare-commit-msg
#
# The mechanism first ruled for #158 was `prepare-commit-msg` keyed on
# `$2 = "commit"`. Measured, that is holed:
#
#     git commit --amend            -> source=[commit]  sha=[HEAD]
#     git commit --amend -m "..."   -> source=[message] sha=[]
#     git commit -m "..."           -> source=[message] sha=[]
#
# An amend WITH `-m` is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a plain commit at that hook --
# and `-m` is the form scripts and agents use, which is almost certainly the
# form that caused the incident. A guard must fire on the incident that
# minted it; that one would have waved it through, for the second time.
#
# `reference-transaction` sees the REF MOVE itself, so the invocation form is
# irrelevant. It also survives `--no-verify` (which bypasses only `pre-commit`
# and `commit-msg`).
#
# ⚠️ CORRECTED 2026-08-08 — THIS BLOCK NAMED THE WRONG PREDICATE. It read:
# *"'Old is not an ancestor of new' is exactly the predicate a remote uses to
# reject a force-push."* That sentence is still TRUE ABOUT REMOTES and was the
# wrong rule for HERE, ruled by Waffles the Terrible and then measured.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE ANCESTOR TEST IS A SPELLING; THE HARM IS ORPHANING COMMITS. They are
# not the same set, and the difference is wrong in BOTH directions:
#   · it REFUSES a harmless move -- retargeting a branch whose old tip is still
#     held by another ref loses nothing, and the ancestor test calls it a rewrite;
#   · it PERMITS the delete that orphans -- `git branch -D` on unmerged work
#     moves no ref non-fast-forward and so never reached the test at all.
# A remote genuinely wants the ancestor rule, because it must protect refs it
# cannot enumerate on a client it does not trust. A local guard CAN enumerate
# every ref, so it can ask the real question: after this transaction, would any
# commit become unreachable? That is what this hook now asks.
#
# TWO SURFACES, TWO PREDICATES -- and that is not a weakening. Class B at the
# remote keeps the ancestor rule for the reason above. Same harm, two fault
# domains, and the domain decides the instrument.
#
# ⛔ THE REPORTED `old` IS NOT THE REF'S OLD VALUE, AND MUST NOT BE USED
#
# git supplies the old OID on stdin ONLY when the caller stated an expected
# old value. Otherwise it sends all-zero. Measured on git 2.47.1:
#
#     git commit --amend                 old=REAL   (refuses correctly)
#     git reset --hard HEAD~1            old=REAL   (refuses correctly)
#     git branch -f <b> <earlier>        old=ZERO   <-- discards commits
#     git update-ref <ref> <earlier>     old=ZERO   <-- discards commits
#     git checkout -B <b> <earlier>      old=ZERO   <-- discards commits
#     git rebase                         old=ZERO   <-- discards commits
#     git branch -D <b>                  old=ZERO, new=ZERO
#
# The first version of this hook read all-zero `old` as "branch creation" and
# allowed it, so it waved through rebase, `branch -f` and `checkout -B` --
# and its own acceptance test passed, because amend and reset happen to sit
# in the half of the class where git does state an expected value. A SAMPLE
# OF THE WORKING HALF IS NOT THE CLASS.
#
# So the ref's real prior value is READ HERE, with `rev-parse`, which at the
# "prepared" state still resolves to the pre-transaction value. That removes
# the dependence on a field that is only sometimes populated, rather than
# trying to interpret it.
#
# ⚠️ DELETION IS NOW IN SCOPE. CORRECTED 2026-08-08, and the block it replaces
# is reproduced because its REASONING was sound and only its INSTRUMENT was
# narrow. It read: *"DELETION IS OUT OF SCOPE, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT AN
# OVERSIGHT"*, on the grounds that `git pack-refs` prunes loose refs by emitting
# `refs/heads/*` deletions through this very hook, so refusing deletion aborts
# `pack-refs` and `git gc`; and that three separations had been tried and failed
# (packed-refs membership, reachability from another ref, the reported `old`).
#
# ⭐⭐ THE STANDING RULE IT ESTABLISHED IS UNCHANGED AND IS WHY THIS IS SAFE:
# A CONTROL THAT CANNOT DISTINGUISH THE HARM FROM ROUTINE MAINTENANCE ON THE
# SAME SURFACE MUST NOT CLAIM THAT HARM. Breaking `git gc` would get this hook
# uninstalled, which is worse than the gap. Nothing below relaxes that; the
# separation is now MEASURED to exist rather than assumed absent.
#
# ⭐ WHAT CHANGED IS THE CONSTRUCT, NOT THE VERDICT ON THE OLD ONE. "Reachability
# from another ref" failed before because it was asked with `--all`, and
# re-measured 2026-08-08 on git 2.47.1 the failure is exact and explainable:
# `--exclude=<ref> --all` cannot suppress HEAD, so on the CHECKED-OUT branch the
# commit about to be orphaned is still reachable from HEAD and the question
# answers "nothing lost" for an amend. The ref set is now built explicitly from
# `for-each-ref`, HEAD is deliberately absent, and every arm then reads correctly.
# A PREDICATE AND THE REF SET YOU ASK IT ABOUT ARE ONE INSTRUMENT; the earlier
# measurement was of a different instrument wearing the same name.
#
# ⛔ WHY HEAD IS OMITTED -- WRITTEN AS THREE CASES ON PURPOSE, BECAUSE THE
# ONE-CASE VERSION INVITES A "SIMPLIFICATION" THAT REOPENS THE HOLE. This comment
# previously read "HEAD is not an independent anchor, it is a pointer AT the ref
# under transaction". CORRECTED by Waffles the Terrible 2026-08-08, who measured
# it: that sentence is true in ONE of the three states and false in another, and a
# reader who believes the one-case reason will helpfully add HEAD back.
#
#   1. HEAD SYMBOLIC TO THE REF UNDER TRANSACTION -- redundant. It resolves to the
#      same tip, so including it adds nothing except the defect above: on the
#      checked-out branch it still holds the PRE-transaction value, which is
#      exactly how an amend came to read as lossless.
#   2. HEAD SYMBOLIC TO SOME OTHER REF -- already covered. That other ref is in
#      the `for-each-ref` set on its own account, so HEAD is a duplicate of it.
#   3. HEAD DETACHED -- ⭐ A GENUINE INDEPENDENT ANCHOR, and omitting it is
#      DELIBERATELY STRICTER. Commits held only by a detached HEAD are treated as
#      unreachable, so a delete that was in fact safe is REFUSED. A false refusal,
#      arguable down by a human in the moment; the alternative failure is a false
#      allow, which is argued down by nobody because nobody sees it.
#
# ⭐ SO THE OMISSION IS NOT "HEAD IS NEVER AN ANCHOR". It is: in two cases HEAD
# adds nothing, in the third it would relax the guard, and NO CASE NEEDS IT.
# Waffles' two-arm measurement, git 2.47.1: with `refs/heads/main` excluded and
# HEAD symbolic to it, the ruled form returned EMPTY (allowed the amend); with
# HEAD detached one commit back and nothing else changed, it returned the commit
# (refused). Same command, opposite verdict, HEAD the only variable.
#
# ⭐ THE PRUNE IS SEPARATED BY SURVIVAL EVIDENCE, NOT BY INTENT. A `pack-refs`
# prune deletes a LOOSE ref whose value git has ALREADY written into
# `packed-refs`, so the ref survives the transaction at the same value. Measured:
#   pack-refs/gc prune      loose=yes  packed-refs holds THIS EXACT value  -> allow
#   branch -D, loose only   loose=yes  no packed entry                     -> refuse
#   branch -D, packed only  loose=no   packed entry                        -> refuse
#   branch -D, both, stale  loose=yes  packed entry DISAGREES with current -> refuse
# The old block's "packed-refs membership" attempt compared MEMBERSHIP; this
# compares membership AND VALUE, which is what makes it a survival test.
#
# ⛔ ONE MEASURED FALSE ALLOW, STATED HERE BECAUSE IT IS A HOLE IN A GUARD AND
# MUST NOT BE DISCOVERED BY SOMEONE RELYING ON IT. `git pack-refs --all
# --no-prune` leaves the loose file in place AND writes packed-refs at the same
# value, which is byte-for-byte the survival signature above. A `git branch -D`
# of unmerged work while a repository sits in that state is WAVED THROUGH.
# Reachable, deliberate, unusual -- `--prune` is the default and plain
# `pack-refs --all` was measured to leave loose=no -- and narrow is not the same
# as closed. It is recorded as open in the #158 findings.
#   ⭐ AND IT IS STILL A STRICT IMPROVEMENT: before this change `branch -D` on
#   unmerged work was unguarded in EVERY state, not just that one.
#
# SCOPE
#
# Fires only when this repository carries the claim ref
# `refs/guards/shared-tree` in its common ref store. Ruled at ablative/docs
# `f3016c7`, amended `15b0fd5`, `09740ee`, `63c6339`, `b63bf0d`.
#
# ⛔ THE CLAIM IS A FACT ABOUT A DEPLOYMENT, NOT ABOUT THE PROJECT: it
# asserts that THIS working tree, ON THIS BOX, is shared by more than one
# seat. A clone is a different working tree. It cannot inherit sharedness --
# only acquire it.
#
# ⭐ SO THE CLAIM MUST NOT TRAVEL, AND THE HOOK MUST. Those are opposite
# requirements and the earlier design ran them together: the marker was a
# TRACKED FILE, which made a local fact travel and handed every laptop clone
# a shared-tree guard for a tree nobody shares. That was a second defect, not
# robustness. A plain `git clone` does not fetch `refs/guards/*` (the default
# refspec is heads-only), and under this ruling THAT IS THE MECHANISM
# WORKING. Claiming a clone is an explicit act on arrival, done as part of
# provisioning.
#
# ⛔ MAKING THE CLAIM TRAVEL IS THEREFORE EXPLICITLY REFUSED. A mirror
# refspec or per-clone fetch config would resurrect claim-about-a-box-you-
# are-not-on AND add a manual step as a load-bearing dependency -- the same
# class as the stale absolute `core.hooksPath` below.
#
# ⛔ A tracked file also forked with every branch: two checkouts of one
# repository could disagree about whether the guard was claimed, while the
# refs they protect are shared. The ref is per-repository by construction,
# so no branch and no worktree can hold a different answer.
#
# ⛔ NOT A GIT-CONFIG KEY EITHER, and not for the reason this block used to
# give. A config key is per-repository too, but it is content-free and
# invisible to git tooling. The ref points at a blob recording seat, date
# and scope, so the claim IS its own citation: a reader who finds a repo
# guarded can ask WHO claimed it and WHY without leaving the repository.
# The ref is equally immune to the failure that motivated the old wording:
# it lives inside the repository, so it moves when the repository moves --
# unlike a stale ABSOLUTE `core.hooksPath`, which is what silently disabled
# hooks in four estate repos after directories moved.
#
# ⛔ WHAT THE REF DOES NOT CARRY IS ITS OWN REMOVAL. MEASURED 2026-08-08:
# default `core.logAllRefUpdates` writes NO reflog for `refs/guards/*`;
# `always` writes one and `git update-ref -d` DESTROYS IT along with the
# ref. Afterwards there is no ref, no `logs/refs/guards/*`, and no mention
# in HEAD's reflog. The blob's self-citation holds for a claim that is
# STILL SET and says nothing about one silently dropped. An earlier draft
# of this block said the ref "carries a reflog"; that was measured false
# and STRUCK at `b63bf0d`. It is recorded here rather than quietly deleted
# because A FALSE PREMISE IN A RULING IS A FUTURE LICENCE TO SKIP THE
# REMEDY IT WAS USED TO JUSTIFY.
#
# ⭐ THE WITNESS IS RELOCATED, NOT LOST. The legitimate disarm of a shared
# repository is AN EDIT TO THE MUST-BE-CLAIMED LIST in the estate ledger --
# a commit: visible, reviewable, attributable, exactly as the tracked file
# was. What IS genuinely lost is act-time detection: the list catches an
# unclaim at the next battery run rather than at the moment of the act, so
# the list's coverage and the battery's cadence are GUARD PROPERTIES now,
# not test hygiene.
#
# KNOWN GAP, STATED NOT HIDDEN
#
# This hook cannot defend a repo it is not installed in, and `.git/hooks` is
# not carried by a clone. Install via a RELATIVE `core.hooksPath` into a
# tracked directory (`git config core.hooksPath .githooks`) -- relative,
# because the four repos that broke did so by being ABSOLUTE. Installation
# remains a manual step and therefore remains the residual gap.
#
# ⭐ NOTE THE ASYMMETRY THIS SECTION EXISTS TO KEEP STRAIGHT: the INSTALL
# must travel (tracked directory, relative path) and the CLAIM must not.
# A change that makes one of them behave like the other is a regression
# even when it looks like an improvement.

set -eu

state="${1:-}"

# Only the "prepared" state can abort the transaction. "committed"/"aborted"
# are notifications and a non-zero exit there means nothing.
[ "$state" = "prepared" ] || exit 0

# THE CLAIM PREDICATE — sourced, never hand-copied. Ruling point 1: this hook
# and check_guard_active.sh shared a defect BY SHARING A PREDICATE THEY EACH
# KEPT BY HAND, so they now share one implementation by construction.
#
# ⛔ A MISSING PREDICATE FILE IS A REFUSAL, NOT A SKIP. Every other failure in
# this file's scope check exits 0, and that is correct for "the guard does not
# apply here". It is CATASTROPHIC for "the guard could not be loaded": those two
# states are indistinguishable to anyone downstream, and the second one is the
# guard being silently absent in a repo that asked for it. Absence ⇒ RAISE.
predicate_dir=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" 2>/dev/null && pwd) || predicate_dir=""
if [ -z "$predicate_dir" ] || [ ! -f "$predicate_dir/claim_predicate.sh" ]; then
    printf '⛔ REFUSED: the class-A guard cannot load its claim predicate.\n' >&2
    printf '   Expected: %s/claim_predicate.sh\n' "${predicate_dir:-<unresolved>}" >&2
    printf '   The hook is installed but INCOMPLETE — claim_predicate.sh must be\n' >&2
    printf '   tracked and shipped alongside this hook, or the guard cannot run.\n' >&2
    printf '   Refusing rather than skipping: a guard that cannot load is not a\n' >&2
    printf '   guard that does not apply, and only one of those is safe to ignore.\n' >&2
    exit 1
fi
# shellcheck source=claim_predicate.sh
. "$predicate_dir/claim_predicate.sh"

# Unclaimed repository ⇒ the guard does not apply here. Ruling point 3: this is
# the LEGITIMATE unclaimed state, not a defect.
claim_is_set || exit 0

# The all-zero OID marks "no value". Its LENGTH differs between sha1 and
# sha256 repositories, so it is detected by "contains no non-zero character"
# rather than by comparing against a hardcoded 40-char literal.
is_zero() {
    case "$1" in
        *[!0]*) return 1 ;;
        '') return 1 ;;
        *) return 0 ;;
    esac
}

# common_dir -- ABSOLUTE. `--git-common-dir` alone returns a path relative to
# the repository, and this hook's cwd is not guaranteed to be the repository
# root, so the relative form silently reads a directory that does not exist and
# every packed-refs probe answers "absent". Measured: that exact substitution
# turned a real false-allow measurement into a meaningless one. The claim ref
# lives in the COMMON dir for the same reason the install does.
common_dir=$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null) || common_dir=""
if [ -z "$common_dir" ] || [ ! -d "$common_dir" ]; then
    printf '⛔ REFUSED: the class-A guard cannot resolve this repository'\''s common\n' >&2
    printf '   git directory, so it cannot tell a pruned ref from a deleted one.\n' >&2
    printf '   Refusing rather than guessing: the guard applies here (the claim is\n' >&2
    printf '   set) and an unanswerable question is not a pass.\n' >&2
    exit 1
fi

# orphans_exist <ref-under-transaction> <positive-rev> [<additional-exclusion>...]
#
# TRUE (0) when at least one commit is reachable from <positive-rev> but from
# NOTHING ELSE that will survive this transaction.
#
# ⭐ THE REF UNDER TRANSACTION IS PASSED, NOT INHERITED. An earlier draft read
# `$ref` straight out of the enclosing loop. That works and it is the same class
# of defect as a hand-kept predicate: correct only while the one caller happens to
# be inside that loop, and silently wrong for the second caller.
#
# ⭐ THE REF SET IS BUILT EXPLICITLY, AND `--all` IS REFUSED ON PURPOSE. `--all`
# feeds in HEAD, `--exclude` cannot suppress it, and HEAD points AT the ref being
# moved -- so `--all` reports an amend as lossless. Measured on git 2.47.1.
#
# Refs are fed through `--stdin` rather than argv: a repository can hold more
# refs than an argument list, and `--stdin` also removes all dependence on word
# splitting, which is not something to rely on in a script other people edit.
#
# NON-COMMIT REFS ARE FILTERED BY OBJECT TYPE, NOT BY NAME, AND THE FILTER IS
# PRECAUTIONARY -- NOT LOAD-BEARING. `refs/guards/shared-tree` points at a BLOB
# (this guard's own claim ref), so the set genuinely contains a non-commit in
# every repository the guard is installed in.
#
# ⚠️ WHAT THIS COMMENT CLAIMED BEFORE, AND IT WAS MY OWN UNMEASURED INFERENCE:
# that feeding a blob to `rev-list` as an exclusion is a FATAL ERROR which would
# make the guard refuse every transaction. MEASURED on git 2.47.1 -- deleting
# this filter leaves the suite at 62/62, and `^refs/guards/shared-tree` on a blob
# returns rc 0, empty stderr, and the correct answer. git tolerates it silently.
# ⭐⭐ I WROTE A FAILURE MODE IN THE GRAMMAR OF A MEASUREMENT WITHOUT TAKING ONE,
# inside the same edit that strikes other people's expired sentences. The mutant
# that survived is what caught it; re-reading the comment never would have.
#
# The filter STAYS, as a deliberate choice with the reason stated honestly: this
# file is a COPY that travels into every guarded repo and runs on whatever git
# that box has, and "an undocumented tolerance in the version I happened to
# measure" is not a property to depend on. Filtering on `%(objecttype)` also
# covers every future non-commit ref, where a name filter would have covered only
# the one ref I knew about. ⛔ But it is defence in depth, and the arm that would
# prove it necessary does not exist -- so do not cite it as one.
orphans_exist() {
    _under="$1"
    _positive="$2"
    shift 2
    {
        printf '%s\n' "$_positive"
        for _extra in "$@"; do
            printf '^%s\n' "$_extra"
        done
        # Every ref except the one under transaction. Exact string compare --
        # a refname is not a pattern and must not be matched as one.
        git for-each-ref --format='%(objecttype) %(refname)' 2>/dev/null |
            while read -r _type _name; do
                case "$_type" in
                    commit|tag) ;;
                    *) continue ;;
                esac
                [ "$_name" = "$_under" ] && continue
                printf '^%s\n' "$_name"
            done
    } | {
        # --max-count=1 : the question is existence, so stop at the first
        # orphan instead of enumerating a potentially huge set.
        _found=$(git rev-list --max-count=1 --stdin 2>/dev/null) || _found=""
        [ -n "$_found" ]
    }
}

# ref_survives_as_packed <ref> <current>
#
# TRUE (0) when `packed-refs` already holds THIS EXACT value for this ref while a
# loose file still exists -- the signature of a `pack-refs`/`gc` prune, where the
# ref survives the transaction unchanged. See the measured table above, including
# the one false allow this cannot separate.
ref_survives_as_packed() {
    [ -f "$common_dir/$1" ] || return 1
    [ -f "$common_dir/packed-refs" ] || return 1
    _packed=$(awk -v r="$1" '$2==r{print $1; exit}' "$common_dir/packed-refs" 2>/dev/null) || _packed=""
    [ -n "$_packed" ] && [ "$_packed" = "$2" ]
}

# refusal_escape -- the sanctioned way out, printed under EVERY refusal.
#
# ⭐ ONE COPY, TWO ARMS. The move arm and the delete arm both need this text, and
# Finding 22 is that two hand-kept copies of one rule drift. It is a function for
# the same reason the claim predicate is a sourced file.
refusal_escape() {
    printf '   If this is genuinely yours to discard, the sanctioned escape is to\n'
    printf '   UNCLAIM the repository deliberately:\n'
    printf '       git update-ref -d refs/guards/shared-tree\n'
    printf '   ⛔ AND SAY SO OUT LOUD, because unlike the old marker THIS LEAVES NO\n'
    printf '   TRACE -- no reflog survives the deletion, so nothing else will ever\n'
    printf '   report that this repo was unguarded. If the unclaiming is permanent,\n'
    printf '   it must also be recorded as an edit to the must-be-claimed list in\n'
    printf '   the estate ledger; that edit is the commit that makes it reviewable:\n'
    printf '       ablative/docs/tracking/must-be-claimed.tsv\n'
    printf '   That edit is the record. Whether anything CHASES you for skipping\n'
    printf '   it depends on wiring that lives outside this repository, so this\n'
    printf '   hook does not tell you -- go and look if it matters to you.\n'
    # ⛔ THE STRUCK SENTENCE SAID "NOTHING WILL CHASE YOU FOR IT ... RIGHT NOW IT
    # IS THE ONLY RECORD THERE WILL EVER BE." RULED OUT ENTIRELY by Waffles the
    # Terrible 2026-08-08 -- STRICKEN, not reworded to name its population,
    # because the sentence had TWO defects and naming the population cures only
    # one.
    #   1. SCOPE. A per-repository hook asserted a fact about THE WHOLE ESTATE.
    #   2. ⭐⭐ EXPIRY. It was true when written and false four hours later in
    #      four repositories -- and this artefact is a COPY that travels into
    #      every guarded repo and outlives the wiring that made it false.
    # ⭐⭐ AN ARTEFACT THAT TRAVELS MUST NOT CARRY A FACT THAT EXPIRES. Keep only
    # what is permanently true: unclaiming leaves no trace, and the sanctioned
    # disarm is a ledger edit. Found by Robo Doug, who rated it fix-first
    # precisely because it is load-bearing text that fires DURING the incident
    # and read as a licence to skip the ledger edit.
}

refused=0
while read -r old new ref; do
    # `old` is deliberately unread -- see the block above. Named for the
    # protocol's shape, discarded on purpose.
    :

    # Local branches only. refs/remotes/* moves on fetch and is not our
    # business; refs/tags, refs/stash, HEAD and the rebase/merge pseudo-refs
    # are handled by their own rules.
    case "$ref" in
        refs/heads/*) ;;
        *) continue ;;
    esac

    # THE REF'S REAL PRIOR VALUE, read rather than trusted. At "prepared" the
    # transaction has not been committed, so this still resolves to the value
    # the ref held before the move. Read BEFORE the deletion arm, because a
    # deletion needs it too -- it is the whole question.
    current=$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$ref^{commit}" 2>/dev/null) || current=""

    # Ref does not exist yet: this is a creation. Nothing can be lost.
    if [ -z "$current" ]; then
        continue
    fi

    # DELETION. In scope as of 2026-08-08 -- see the measured block above.
    if is_zero "$new"; then
        # A prune whose value is already safe in packed-refs is routine
        # maintenance: the ref survives unchanged. Allowed FIRST, so that
        # `git gc` and `git pack-refs` cannot be broken by the check below.
        if ref_survives_as_packed "$ref" "$current"; then
            continue
        fi
        if orphans_exist "$ref" "$current"; then
            printf '⛔ REFUSED: deleting %s would ORPHAN COMMITS in a shared working tree.\n' "$ref" >&2
            printf '   tip: %s\n' "$current" >&2
            printf '   Commits reachable only from this branch are held by no other ref,\n' >&2
            printf '   so deleting it discards them -- and in a shared tree they may be\n' >&2
            printf '   another seat'\''s. `git branch -d` would have refused this; `-D`\n' >&2
            printf '   overrides that refusal, and this guard does not honour the override.\n' >&2
            printf '   If the work is genuinely finished with, merge it or tag it first.\n' >&2
            refusal_escape >&2
            refused=1
        fi
        continue
    fi

    # Resolve the incoming value. A `new` that will not resolve to a commit
    # (a symref payload such as "ref:refs/heads/x", or a corrupt OID) cannot
    # be proved safe, and unprovable means refused -- the safe direction.
    target=$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$new^{commit}" 2>/dev/null) || target=""
    if [ -z "$target" ]; then
        printf '⛔ REFUSED: %s -- incoming value %s does not resolve to a commit,\n' "$ref" "$new" >&2
        printf '   so this move cannot be shown to preserve history.\n' >&2
        refused=1
        continue
    fi

    # THE PREDICATE. Not "is the old tip an ancestor of the new one" -- that is a
    # spelling of the harm, and it is wrong in both directions (see the corrected
    # block at the top). The question is whether this move leaves any commit
    # reachable from NOTHING: the incoming value is offered as an anchor, every
    # other ref is offered as an anchor, HEAD is not (it points at this ref).
    #
    # Refuses amend, reset backwards, rebase, branch -f and checkout -B whenever
    # they discard work. ALLOWS a retarget whose old tip another ref still holds,
    # which the ancestor test refused for no reason. In a shared tree there is no
    # ORPHANING move safe to wave through, because HEAD's author tells you NOTHING
    # about whose work it is -- git identity does not vary across seats on one box.
    if orphans_exist "$ref" "$current" "$target"; then
        printf '⛔ REFUSED: %s would ORPHAN COMMITS in a shared working tree.\n' "$ref" >&2
        printf '   %s -> %s\n' "$current" "$target" >&2
        printf '   Commits reachable only from the current tip are held by no other\n' >&2
        printf '   ref, so this move discards them -- and in a shared tree they may be\n' >&2
        printf '   another seat'\''s. A correction is a NEW COMMIT.\n' >&2
        refusal_escape >&2
        refused=1
    fi
done

exit "$refused"
